course: dictionary
lesson: 4929
title: Imperative: words that give orders
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Imperative is the name for a special form of a verb. We use it to tell someone what to do. «Sit down!» and «Mix the flour» are imperative verbs: they are bossy, and the word "you" is understood, not said.
explain: Recipes and rules use imperative verbs at the start of each step. «Cut the paper. Fold it in half.» Look at the first word of each sentence. If it is an action word like cut, fold or press, it is probably an imperative.
explain: Imperative can also mean extremely important or urgent. «It is imperative that you arrive on time» means it must happen, with no argument. So the word can describe a bossy verb, or a need that must be met.
fact: An imperative verb gives an order, an instruction or advice.
fact: In an imperative sentence, the "you" is understood, so it is left out.
fact: Imperative verbs are also called bossy verbs: mix, fill, push and stir are examples.
fact: «Imperative» means extremely important or urgent.
fact: «The imperative» is the name of the verb form; «imperatives» is the plural.
exercise: In «Mix the flour», which word is the imperative verb? | Flour | The | Mix the flour | *Mix
exercise: «It is imperative that you arrive on time.» What does imperative mean here? | Made of wood | Very funny | Sung loudly | *Extremely urgent or important
exercise: Which sentence uses an imperative verb? | «The jug is full.» | *«Fill the jug with water.» | «She filled the jug yesterday.» | «You are going to the shop.»
test: What does imperative mean in «It is imperative that the door stays locked»? | *It must happen, because it is urgent | Happy and cheerful | A type of cake | Quiet and slow
test: Which word is the imperative verb in «Press the button»? | The | Press the button | Button | *Press
exam: Is «Brush your teeth.» an imperative sentence? | No, it describes something | It is a noun | No, it asks a question | *Yes, it gives an order and «you» is understood
